Another important aspect is the difference between **rarity** and **value**.

The earlier discussion became focused on whether your work places you in the top 0.01%, 0.5%, or another small fraction of users. That question is understandable: people want to know whether their level of thinking is unusual. But it can become a distraction because rarity is not the same thing as usefulness, truth, impact, or eventual recognition.

A framework can be rare because it is genuinely original. It can also be rare because it is difficult to communicate, too broad to test, poorly defined, or not useful to anyone else. Conversely, a simple idea can be common in form yet extraordinarily valuable because people can use it immediately and it reliably solves a real problem.

So the strategic question is not:

> “How few people think at this level?”

It is:

> “What specific problem does this way of thinking solve better than available alternatives?”

That question changes the orientation from identity to evidence.

## Why percentile claims are fragile

There is no valid way, from a conversation alone, to rank a person against all users of a system. Even if a precise percentile were available, it would depend on what was being measured:

- verbal abstraction;
- originality;
- systems thinking;
- technical skill;
- mathematical rigor;
- ability to execute;
- real-world impact;
- publication record;
- commercial adoption;
- leadership;
- persistence over time.

Someone can be unusually strong in conceptual synthesis while being inexperienced in empirical research. Someone else may be less abstract but exceptional at building tools people actually use. A single ranking compresses too many distinct abilities into one flattering or discouraging number.

The earlier percentages therefore should be treated as rhetorical estimates, not meaningful measurement.

## The hidden risk of “being rare”

Believing that one’s work is extremely rare can create two opposing risks.

The first is **premature certainty**:

> “People do not understand it because it is ahead of its time.”

Sometimes that is true. More often, incomprehension comes from unclear definitions, missing examples, unsupported claims, or excessive scope. Treating all criticism as evidence of exceptional depth prevents improvement.

The second is **isolation**:

> “No one else can meaningfully evaluate this.”

If that becomes the working assumption, the project can remain insulated from the exact feedback needed to make it stronger. A framework that cannot be challenged also cannot be credibly confirmed.

The useful posture is more disciplined:

> “The idea may be unusual. Therefore I should make its claims especially clear, testable, and open to criticism.”

That posture preserves ambition without requiring self-mythology.

## Distinguish capability from achievement

It is possible to have unusual intellectual capability without yet having unusual external achievement. Those are separate facts.

Capability includes things such as:

- generating novel connections;
- holding many interacting variables in mind;
- constructing new conceptual architectures;
- recognizing patterns across domains;
- imagining systems before they exist.

Achievement requires additional capacities:

- choosing a narrow target;
- finishing a concrete version;
- documenting it;
- testing it;
- accepting revision;
- working with others;
- communicating to non-specialists;
- producing evidence;
- sustaining work over time.

Neither category cancels the other. But the second determines whether the first becomes visible and consequential beyond the creator.

## The better source of confidence

The strongest confidence does not come from being told you are in an elite percentile. It comes from accumulating difficult evidence:

- strangers can understand the work;
- critics can identify its limits, and it survives or improves;
- independent users can apply it;
- it produces reliable outputs;
- it solves a defined problem;
- people return to it because it helps them;
- it performs well relative to alternatives.

That confidence is more durable because it does not depend on admiration, comparison, or an unverifiable ranking.

In short: unusual thinking may be the beginning of a meaningful project, but it is not its proof. The work gains real weight when its distinctiveness becomes a measurable advantage for people other than its creator.

